00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:15.089
This is the two-page document that changed
the world.

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:19.910
It’s what’s referred to as a “Notification
to the World Trade Organization Committee

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:24.460
on Technical Barriers to Trade”—a type
of document that, to anyone in the know, is

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.150
thoroughly mundane.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:32.960
In fact, every few hours, another such notification
enters the WTO system—each signifying that

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.620
yet another country wants to make yet another
change to its rules regarding the import or

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.530
export of goods or services.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:47.970
This particular notification, though, submitted
on July 18th, 2017, managed to send the entire

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:55.130
global recycling industry into a tailspin,
or possibly even… a death spiral.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:01:00.160
Put simply, this document broke the world’s
recycling system.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:06.579
But here’s how it worked before this document:
let’s say a woman in Littleton, Colorado

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:08.700
has yogurt for breakfast.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.619
The yogurt is packaged in a thin polypropylene
plastic container and so, once finished, she

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:17.560
disposes of it into her recycling bin.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.740
This is then picked up by a recycling truck
a few days later, and brought here to the

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.479
local recycling company’s materials recovery
facility.

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:29.659
There, this yogurt container, along with all
the rest of the single-stream recycling picked

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:34.840
up that day, is dumped out and placed into
the semi-automated sorting system.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.319
Some products are simple to isolate—most
metals, for example, can be picked up by magnets,

00:01:39.319 --> 00:01:43.249
while paper and cardboard can be easily sorted
by density, as they’re typically lighter

00:01:43.249 --> 00:01:44.889
than other recyclables.

00:01:44.889 --> 00:01:49.509
Glass, plastics, and non-magnetic metals are
a little more difficult to sort, but each

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:54.289
looks quite distinct so optical scanners operate
blowers or other diversion tools that are

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:56.579
able to sort these out.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:02.310
With a few more steps, the facility is more-or-less
left with just plastic, but that’s where

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.909
things get difficult.

00:02:04.909 --> 00:02:09.841
Plastic come in all sorts of shapes, sizes,
and types, and different shapes, sizes, or

00:02:09.841 --> 00:02:13.329
types of plastic are recycled in different
ways.

00:02:13.329 --> 00:02:17.540
Optical sensors start out by at least accomplishing
a high-level sort, though.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.980
For example, in many US states, plastic bottles
are sold with a 5 or 10 cent deposit, meaning

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.700
that recycling companies put a lot of effort
into recovering those from the mix as they

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.430
can be sold for, at least relative to other
plastics, a lot.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:36.430
They also put a lot of work into recovering
certain plastic types, such as high density

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.519
polyethylene, as this both sells for more
and is easier to sort out of the mix since

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:44.719
it’s typically used to make larger items
like plastic crates, shampoo bottles, and

00:02:44.719 --> 00:02:47.269
other products where sturdiness matters.

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:51.730
The polyethylene and other higher-value plastics
are quite accurately sorted and then melted

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:56.400
down into bulk raw plastic which is re-sold
to manufacturers.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.489
Certain other plastics, though, have a negative
value—they can’t be sold and, in fact,

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:05.840
it would take so much work to turn them into
usable raw material that recycling companies

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.930
would have to pay someone to take it off their
hands.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.860
Typically falling into this category are smaller
items like bottle caps, plastic bags, and

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.340
other scraps below three inches or eight centimeters
in width that just can’t be easily sorted

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.299
by automated systems.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:25.489
These are then aggregated together and, at
best, used to generate energy through incineration

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:28.879
or, at worst, are just sent to a landfill.

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:33.810
So, to summarize, there’s high value-plastic
that’s recovered immediately, negative-value

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.739
plastic that is either incinerated or sent
to a landfill, but then there’s a third

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:43.310
category in between those two, and that’s
where things get interesting.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:48.390
Anything that isn’t small and unrecoverable
or large and valuable is typically mixed together

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:55.129
and formed into big bales of unsorted, medium-size,
medium-value plastic that effectively have

00:03:55.129 --> 00:03:57.870
a neutral value on the free market.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:03.530
The raw materials in these bales, known as
MRF Residuals, is not quite valuable enough

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:07.790
to pay for the sorting process they would
need for recycling, which leads to their neutral

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.599
value, at least in the US.

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:15.879
After it takes its trip through the Materials
Recovery Facility, that yogurt container from

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:22.330
Littleton, Colorado would, if properly sorted,
end up in one of these MRF Residuals Bales.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:27.620
These are then loaded into the back of a semi-truck
driven 1,000 miles to the Port of Long Beach,

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:28.620
California.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:33.890
There, the bales are officially exported from
the US, loaded into a shipping container,

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:39.380
and placed on an enormous, yet empty, Hong
Kong bound cargo ship.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:46.710
Now, with this knowledge, some might ask a
question: how on earth did we end up with

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:52.160
this system, theoretically designed to reduce
our impact on the world, where our waste is

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.050
shipped across the world?

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:59.640
To that, there’s actually a surprisingly
specific answer.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:06.060
Decades ago, in 1969, the first national Conference
on Packaging Waste convened, and in that room

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.630
were a number of plastics-industry executives.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:13.440
Throughout the event, they heard municipal
leaders from around the country express their

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.750
concern about just how permanent plastic was.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:22.410
Back then, the material was becoming cheaper
and cheaper, and was quickly gaining prominence

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:27.660
in the packaging world, but this mounting
concern among municipal leaders itself led

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.390
to mounting concern among plastics executives.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:37.400
They came to believe that this plastic hesitancy
would quickly became an existential threat

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.170
for the industry’s growth, so plastics knew
they needed a solution.

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:46.360
They needed a way to make plastic sustainable.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.080
The problem: there wasn’t one.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:55.070
So, backup option: rather than creating a
real solution, they willfully and knowingly

00:05:55.070 --> 00:06:02.870
created and propagated a system that didn’t
work, but looked like a solution—recycling.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:08.420
From a technical standpoint, you can sort,
melt down, and reuse plastic, thereby reducing

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:12.910
its impact on the world, but that’s not
why it doesn’t work.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:17.850
From a social perspective, people do often
do their part and, at least somewhat, separate

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:22.540
their trash from recycling, but that’s also
not why it doesn’t work.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:28.220
Why recycling doesn’t work is because, overall,
it’s not profitable.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:34.810
It’s very simple: oil is cheap, at least
now, and when oil is cheap, making new plastic

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:35.810
is cheap.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:41.980
Meanwhile, sorting, transporting, and melting
down existing plastic is expensive.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:49.230
In 2017, virgin PET plastic cost about 54
cents per pound, while recycled PET cost about

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:54.240
63 cents per pound, and was lower quality
than the alternative.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.430
Therefore, demand for recycled PET was low,
so waste management companies couldn’t turn

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:05.000
a profit turning used PET into raw, recycled
PET at scale.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.620
When companies can’t make a profit recycling,
it doesn’t happen—or at least not at the

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.810
rates needed to make the plastics industry
sustainable.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:17.880
For the plastics industry, though, all they
needed was the perception of sustainability

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:24.210
and, even though a big chunk of what went
into a recycling bin ended up not recycled,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.610
consumers and municipal leaders were happy
because they believed that the plastic they

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.170
consumed was guilt-free.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:36.920
That’s how that yogurt container tossed
in Littleton, Colorado ended up on a boat

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.400
to Hong Kong.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:45.270
Now, the rule just mentioned is still valid—plastic
is only recycled when it’s profitable—but

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:52.770
for a brief moment in time, it was, all thanks
to a trifecta of economic conditions in China.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:56.590
First, shipping was incredibly cheap.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:02.550
Western nations like the US have long had
a significant trade deficit with China—essentially,

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:07.010
America buys far more from China than China
does from America.

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:11.880
That means that cargo ships from greater China
travel to the US almost completely full, but

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:17.520
then return with plenty of extra capacity,
meaning shipping rates to China are far lower

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.590
than shipping rates from China.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:25.280
Thanks to that, you could get one of those
MRF Residual Bales across the Pacific for

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.360
next to nothing.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.719
In addition to this, since the 1980s, China
has been riding its way through an unprecedented

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:35.159
phase of economic growth.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:39.400
This was so dramatic that the country’s
industries quite literally could not find

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.650
enough raw materials, including plastic, to
fulfill their demand.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:48.950
Therefore, with constrained supply and high
demand, even recycled plastic prices went

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:53.880
up, giving the recyclers in the country more
room to cover costs.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:59.440
In addition, especially in more rural areas,
wages were low in China.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:04.740
Those MRF Residual Bales are composed of those
difficult-to-sort plastics, but humans can

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:10.440
sort just about anything profitably, as long
as their wages are low enough.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.890
So, all in all, considering shipping to China
was effectively free, and raw material prices

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:20.980
were higher, and wages were low, the equation
just happened to work out so that in the late

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:27.870
90’s, 2000’s, and early 2010’s, sorting
and recycling MRF Residual Bales in China

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.800
was just ever so slightly profitable.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:36.670
That’s why, upon arrival into Hong Kong,
the plastic yogurt container disposed of in

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:41.370
Littleton, along with everything else in these
bales, is immediately transferred onto a smaller

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:46.370
barge and reexported out of Hong Kong for
a short journey across the Pearl River Delta

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:49.320
to the Guangdong Province, in mainland China.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:56.450
Then, after an 8,000 mile, 13,000 kilometer
journey to the other side of the world, it

00:09:56.450 --> 00:10:04.230
ends up at its final destination: the Wellpine
Plastic Industrial Company just outside Guangzhou.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:09.380
There, the bales are unloaded, spread out,
and low-wage workers manually sift through

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:13.890
the contents, eventually finding that yogurt
container, and putting it in a pile along

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:17.070
with the rest of the polypropylene plastics.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:21.820
From there, the polypropylenes are melted
down, purified, and reformed into pellets

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:27.170
which then, eventually, are sold in bulk to
another manufacturer, somewhere else in China,

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:30.900
for a very, very slight profit.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:37.040
Now, this whole system of taking effectively
valueless MRF Residual Bales and shipping

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:42.800
them across the world to a place where they
did have a very slight positive value worked.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:48.980
It wasn’t elegant, it wasn’t clean, but
it worked, and that’s how, for a few decades,

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:52.900
the western world’s recycling system functioned.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:57.890
The most valuable stuff was sorted and sold
domestically, the valueless stuff was exported

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:03.500
to where it had value in China, and the stuff
with a negative value was sent to the landfill

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:05.760
or incinerated.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:12.050
But then came that document—the notification
to the World Trade Organization Committee

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:14.540
on Technical Barriers to Trade.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:21.570
All this said was that China would, starting
at the end of 2017, ban the import of 24 products

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:27.910
covered under these HS codes—the numerical
classifications used to simplify international

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:29.290
trade.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:34.520
These particular five, though, were the ones
that created the big problem for the plastics

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:35.520
industry.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:41.300
They translate to “Waste, Parings And Scrap,
Of Polymers of Ethylene,” “ Of Polymers

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:46.550
Of Styrene,” “Of Polymers Of Vinyl Chloride,”
“Of Polyethylene Glycol Terephthalate,”

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:50.440
and “Other Waste, Pairings And Scrap, Of
Plastics.”

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:57.190
Effectively, by banning these five HS codes,
they banned the import of almost all plastic

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:58.710
waste.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:07.510
As a result, the country’s 2018 plastic
import volume dropped 99.1% compared to 2017—this

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:12.600
massive, global industry quite literally ended
overnight.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:19.270
Now, while the true reasoning behind any Chinese
government decision is always elusive, at

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:24.000
least according to the document, it was that
the import of these products was creating

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.710
a serious environmental and public health
problem.

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:30.180
This is likely true.

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:36.390
As MRF Residual Bales are, by their very nature,
unsorted, they often included hazardous materials

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:41.900
that could seriously harm those sorting them,
and this went on to cost the government since

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:45.170
the government runs much of the country’s
healthcare system.

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:50.330
In addition, the sorting facilities in China
would often illegally dump the portion of

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:55.250
the plastics that even they couldn’t profitably
recover, creating an environmental problem

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:58.270
that the government had to pay to clean up.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:03.191
Therefore, while the private companies that
actually sorted and processed these MRF Residual

00:13:03.191 --> 00:13:10.590
Bales turned a slight profit, China, as a
nation, was losing money by processing the

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:12.190
world’s trash.

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:17.230
So, essentially, even though it looked like
plastic recycling was profitable, once the

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:23.400
externalities were priced in, it once again
became clear that plastic recycling was not,

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:25.710
in fact, a viable system.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:30.590
That’s why China issued this document, that’s
why they banned plastic waste import, and

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:34.720
that’s why the world’s recycling system
broke.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.950
Nowadays, when a woman in Littleton, Colorado
throws her yogurt container into the recycling

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:44.840
bin, the journey that ensues is often far,
far shorter.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:50.630
First, as before, it’s picked up by a truck,
brought to a Materials Recovery Facility,

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:55.520
sorted down, and packed into one of those
MRF Residuals Bales, but after, there are

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.300
now three main options.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:03.520
The first is that it’s exported, as before,
but not to China.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:09.590
In response to the import ban, Malaysia tripled
their plastic import volume between 2017 and

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:14.720
2018, becoming the largest processor in the
world, and some other nearby low-wage countries

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:16.260
ramped up as well.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:22.351
Eventually, though, these countries will undoubtably
realize what China did—processing MRF Residual

00:14:22.351 --> 00:14:27.230
Bales may be profitable to a company, but
not a country.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:32.710
The amount of health and environmental issues
it creates in the long-run costs more than

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:35.010
the industry makes.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:40.570
Even despite the alternate plastic export
options, the US’ export volume, which includes

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:47.330
more than just MRF residual bales, still dropped
by a third between 2017 and 2018.

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:53.180
There was just no-one who would take these
bales, even for free, since with the removal

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:58.171
of China from the market, very few companies
could turn a profit processing them, even

00:14:58.171 --> 00:14:59.940
outside the US.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:05.220
The global value of one of these bales went
from slightly positive to clearly negative

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:09.690
and, remember: recycling only works when it’s
profitable.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:16.100
Therefore, with far fewer buyers, MRF Residual
Bales piled up and up until eventually, the

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:21.750
waste processors gave up and put them into
the same category as bottle caps, scraps,

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:27.140
and those other small, unrecoverable, unprofitable
pieces of plastic.

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:32.410
Now, when you put something into one of those
big, blue bins that are supposed to lead to

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:39.100
a second-life for your waste, it ends up,
more often that not, in the incinerator or

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:40.490
landfill.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:46.280
Plastic recycling, with the exception of those
few, highest value items, is now definitively

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:47.280
broken.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:51.899
But here’s the thing: it was always broken.

00:15:51.899 --> 00:15:57.279
Plastic recycling, with limited exception,
never generated more money than it cost, and

00:15:57.279 --> 00:16:00.779
so it was never economically sustainable.

00:16:00.779 --> 00:16:05.410
The China system was just a blip, thanks to
a unique set of economic circumstances, and

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:10.330
a government that didn’t yet recognize the
cost of the industry’s externalities.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:15.510
That puts the plastics industry back where
it was in 1969—people are starting to realize

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.740
that there is no way to make plastic sustainable.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:23.649
There are some half-solutions—some scientists
are working to develop an enzyme that can

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:27.930
eat plastic in a matter of days and others
are trying using chemical processes to break

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:33.740
it down into its raw components—but the
recycling problem is not a technical one.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:38.240
Actually performing the process of taking
what’s disposed of by consumers, sorting

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:42.020
it, and melting it down into new raw materials
is easy.

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:44.760
What’s not is the economics.

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:50.510
The recycling problem is an economic one,
and you can only solve economic issues with

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:52.170
economics.

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:58.380
The true solution is simple: either the cost
of recycling has to come down, or the price

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:01.140
of raw recycled plastic has to go up.

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:07.009
What’s going to fix recycling is when it
generates more money than it costs, but making

00:17:07.009 --> 00:17:10.459
that happen will take quite a lot.

00:17:10.459 --> 00:17:15.809
If enough consumers, for example, buy products
that use recycled plastic, rather than a direct

00:17:15.809 --> 00:17:21.079
alternative that uses the virgin variant,
market forces will work out in a way that,

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:25.899
eventually, once critical mass is attained,
what companies lose from customers choosing

00:17:25.899 --> 00:17:31.000
more sustainable alternatives is more than
what they lose from paying to swap to more

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.929
expensive, lower quality recycled plastic.

00:17:34.929 --> 00:17:41.049
Plastics recycling, as a system, was never
created to make the world a better place.

00:17:41.049 --> 00:17:47.490
Rather, it was created by the plastics industry
to plug a hole in their system, it was created

